\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces Automatic mechanism for temple door actuation designed by Heron of Alexandria (62~CE). When the altar fire is lit, the air in cavity~(E) expands thermally, pressurising the water-filled reservoir~(H) and forcing water through pipe~(L) into cauldron~(M). The resulting weight displacement overcomes counterweight~(R), opening the doors; the process reverses when the fire is extinguished. Adapted from~\blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {herondoor}.}}{2}{figure.caption.6}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.2}{\ignorespaces Seismoscope developed by Zhang Heng (132~CE). A central pendulum oscillates in response to seismic vibrations, triggering a lever that releases a bronze ball from one of eight dragon heads into the mouth of the corresponding frog below. The direction of the activated pair indicates the primary orientation of the earthquake epicenter. Adapted from~\blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {seismoscope}.}}{3}{figure.caption.7}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.3}{\ignorespaces Example of a batch-fabricated semiconductor wafer. Parallel manufacturing yields hundreds of identical micro-devices simultaneously, significantly lowering per-unit production costs.}}{3}{figure.caption.8}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.4}{\ignorespaces Structural representation of a MEMS device. Microstructures typically exhibit characteristic dimensions on the order of micrometers~\blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {birkholz2013}.}}{4}{figure.caption.9}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.5}{\ignorespaces Essential elements of an atom-based sensor: a laser light source, an atomic vapor cell, and a photodiode detector. A resonant beam passes through the cell; the transmitted (or fluorescent) light carries information about the atomic quantum state and the physical quantity of interest. Adapted from~\cite {brown2025}.}}{4}{figure.caption.10}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.6}{\ignorespaces Optical micrograph of a fabricated LWVC showing its internal architecture: (a)~reservoir chamber, (b)~sensing channel, (c)~micro-strainer conduits, and (d)~overall structural outline. All cavities are inscribed in a single monolithic fused-silica block by the FLICE process. The overall size of the cell is \SI {5}{\milli \meter }$\times $\SI {10}{\milli \meter }, and the size of the microchannels is in the micrometer range.}}{8}{figure.caption.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.7}{\ignorespaces Optical micrograph of the identical LWVC configuration following the physical integration of the NEG dispenser within the reservoir, captured prior to the optical contact bonding stage. The visible defects on the sample are a consequence of extensive prototyping and handling tests, and do not reflect the nominal fabrication quality.}}{9}{figure.caption.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.8}{\ignorespaces Conceptual illustration of an Atomic-Photonic Integrated Circuit. The hybrid device couples a Laser-Written Vapor Cell to external optical components via dedicated interfaces. The working principle is as follows: the NEG dispenser in the reservoir (a) is activated, letting the gas flow through the microchannel (b) to the sensing chambers (c). A specialized waveguide coupler (d) links the sensing region with an external photonic waveguide or fiber assembly (e), creating a functional hybrid atomic chip. Adapted from QSENSATO Srl \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {qsensato_web}.}}{10}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.9}{\ignorespaces Conceptual rendering of a femtosecond laser-written waveguide network embedded within a monolithic glass substrate. This maskless technique enables the direct 3D routing of light, establishing a technological foundation for fully monolithic atomic-photonic integration. Adapted from QSENSATO Srl \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {qsensato_web}.}}{11}{figure.caption.15}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.10}{\ignorespaces Wafer-scale fabrication of LWVCs: multiple devices inscribed simultaneously in a single fused-silica substrate by QSENSATO~Srl. All subsequent etching, filling, and bonding steps are performed at the wafer level, enabling cost-effective batch production of chip-scale quantum sensors. Image courtesy of QSENSATO~Srl~\blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {qsensato_web}.}}{12}{figure.caption.16}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Fabrication worklow of a Laser-Written Vapor Cell. Steps (1-2) describe the FLICE process. In step (4) NEG dispenser placement and bonding are performed. Activation is illustrated by step (5). From \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {lucivero2022}.}}{15}{figure.caption.17}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces Laser-Written Vapor Cell manufactured using the FLICE technique. It consists of a cylindrical reservoir (5~mm diameter, 3.5~mm height) connected to a rectangular parallelepiped sensing channel ($1 \times 1$~mm cross-section, 9.5~mm length). From \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {lucivero2022}}}{19}{figure.caption.22}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.3}{\ignorespaces Hydroxyl radicals formation upon the decomposition of Caro's acid in Piranha solution. From \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {caro2017}.}}{23}{figure.caption.27}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.4}{\ignorespaces Schematic representation of a hydroxylated/hydrophilic surface. From \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {kern1993}.}}{23}{figure.caption.28}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.5}{\ignorespaces Schematic representation of a contaminant being lifted from a quartz surface due to the Zeta potential. Adapted from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {lin2012}.}}{24}{figure.caption.30}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.6}{\ignorespaces Effects of plasma treatment on silica surfaces. Plasma removes contaminants and increases the density of available silanol groups, while generating a thin less dense, nanoporous subsurface layer, increasing the diffusivity of water and trapped gases at the interface. Adapted from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {yu2023}.}}{26}{figure.caption.34}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.7}{\ignorespaces Bonding energy as a function of plasma treatment duration, highlighting the transition from optimal surface activation to degradation via overetching after \SI {60}{\second } of exposure. Adapted from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {li2013}.}}{27}{figure.caption.35}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.8}{\ignorespaces Evolution of bonding energy as a function of annealing temperature. Adapted from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {li2013}.}}{28}{figure.caption.36}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.9}{\ignorespaces Evolution of bonding energy with respect to annealing time. The bond strength follows a logarithmic growth law, where thermal annealing reduces the characteristic time constant $\mu $ to accelerate convergence toward maximum bond strength. From \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {cho2001}.}}{29}{figure.caption.37}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.10}{\ignorespaces Effect of applied pressure on the bonding process. Forces perpendicular to the interface suppress void formation by closing residual gaps between surface asperities. From \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {mai2015}.}}{30}{figure.caption.38}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.11}{\ignorespaces Pressure plate geometry described by Birckigt et al.\ \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {birckigt2021}. The curved profiles guide contact front propagation outward from the center, reducing the probability of interface void formation.}}{31}{figure.caption.39}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Example of the probe of an Atomic Force Microscope. From \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {wiki_afm_tip}.}}{34}{figure.caption.42}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces 2D Surface images obtained through AFM in three different parts of the pristine Nanoquartz disk's surface.}}{35}{figure.caption.43}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces 3D Surface images obtained through AFM in three different parts of the pristine Nanoquartz disk's surface.}}{36}{figure.caption.44}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces Surface roughness distribution obtained through AFM. It can be seen that most of the defects are below the \SI {1}{\nano \meter } range. Furthermore, the mean ($\mu $) is close to zero, confirming that the surface is uniform.}}{36}{figure.caption.45}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.5}{\ignorespaces Geometrical definition of the contact angle $\theta _C$ at the three-phase boundary between a liquid droplet, the ambient gas, and the solid substrate. The equilibrium value is set by the balance of interfacial energies expressed in Young's equation (Eq.~\eqref {eq:young}). From \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {wikicontact}.}}{37}{figure.caption.47}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.6}{\ignorespaces Representative contact angle regimes across different wettability states. Surface~A ($\theta _C > 90^\circ $) is hydrophobic; surfaces~B, C, and~D are sorted from partially to fully hydrophilic ($\theta _C \to 0^\circ $). From \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {wikicontacts}.}}{38}{figure.caption.48}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.7}{\ignorespaces Examples of Newton rings formed in the interphase of fused silica disks.}}{39}{figure.caption.49}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.8}{\ignorespaces Illustration of the razor blade method. A razor blade is inserted between the bonded surfaces to quantify the bonding energy between them. From \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {mack1997}.}}{40}{figure.caption.50}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.9}{\ignorespaces Real execution of the razor blade method. The de-bonded area can be identified by the presence of a Newton ring (light grey area). Adapted from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {gabriel2006}.}}{40}{figure.caption.51}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.10}{\ignorespaces Schematic representation of water stress corrosion at the hydrophilic bonding interface during debonding under controlled atmospheres. Left: wet atmosphere showing ambient water attack on \ce {Si-O-Si} bonds. Center: anhydrous (dry) atmosphere with reduced water availability. Right: vacuum conditions with partial desorption of interfacial water. Adapted from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {takeuchi2022}.}}{41}{figure.caption.52}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.11}{\ignorespaces Schematic diagram of the tensile test setup for bonding strength measurement. The bonded wafer pair is glued to metal fixtures and subjected to perpendicular tensile force. Adapted from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {gosele2006}.}}{42}{figure.caption.53}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.12}{\ignorespaces Energy level diagram showing the D1 and D2 transitions in an alkali metal atom. From \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {shi2019}}}{44}{figure.caption.54}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Experimental setup at UniBa, showing the chemical hood and the ultrasonic cleaner utilized for the controlled cleaning protocols.}}{48}{figure.caption.56}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Oxygen (\ce {O2}) plasma treatment of the fused silica disks. The activated plasma gas imparts a characteristic purple glow to the interior of the vacuum chamber during the surface modification process.}}{49}{figure.caption.58}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Experimental equipment: (a) extensive drying of the fused silica disks utilizing a high-pressure nitrogen (\ce {N2}) gun; (b) alignment fixture utilized to accurately position and hold the disks.}}{50}{figure.caption.62}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces Bonding results for procedures with two samples: Ethanol+DIW (top), Plasma-Only (middle), and DIW+Piranha+DIW+Plasma (bottom). Each row displays Sample 1 (Raw/Fiji) followed by Sample 2 (Raw/Fiji).}}{51}{figure.caption.63}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces Bonding results for chemical procedures with three independent samples: DIW+Piranha+DIW (P, top lines) and RCA-1+Piranha (R, bottom lines). Raw photographs are paired side-by-side with their Fiji binary maps.}}{52}{figure.caption.64}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.6}{\ignorespaces Custom 3D-printed fixture designed to securely hold the fused silica disks during the tensile test without inducing shear stress.}}{53}{figure.caption.65}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.7}{\ignorespaces Experimental setup for the tensile test, showing the bonded fused silica disks mounted within the 3D-printed fixtures and attached to the testing machine.}}{53}{figure.caption.66}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.8}{\ignorespaces Schematic layout of a radio-frequency OPM. The alkali vapor is spin-polarized along the static field ($B_{\mathrm {DC}}$) by a $\sigma ^+$ pump beam. An orthogonal AC magnetic field excites the Larmor precession, which rotates the polarization plane of a perpendicular, linearly polarized probe beam. This optical rotation is detected by a polarimeter and analyzed using a lock-in amplifier. Adapted from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {deans2021}.}}{55}{figure.caption.68}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.9}{\ignorespaces OPM imaging of (a) a \SI {37}{\milli \meter }$\times $\SI {37}{\milli \meter } square, (b) an isosceles triangle with one side of \SI {37}{\milli \meter } and two sides of \SI {30}{\milli \meter }, and (c) a disk with a \SI {37}{\milli \meter } diameter. Adapted from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {wickenbrock2014}.}}{56}{figure.caption.69}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.10}{\ignorespaces Geometric configuration of the 2D COMSOL simulation domain, illustrating the spatial distribution of (a) the rail profile, measuring approximately \SI {17}{\centi \meter } in width and \SI {20}{\centi \meter } in height, (b) the mechanical fastening clamps, (c) the localized foot corrosion defect, (d) the overhead AC excitation coils, (e) the bounding air region, (f) the underlying granite foundation ballast, and (g) the outermost Infinite Element Domain layer. The spatial evaluation cutpoint for the OPM sensor is constrained above the mechanical clearance line.}}{58}{figure.caption.71}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.11}{\ignorespaces Finite-element mesh discretization showing (a) the global domain overview, (b) localized refinement near the excitation coils, (c) detail of the rail foot assembly, and (d) high-resolution inflation layers tracking the corrosion boundaries.}}{59}{figure.caption.72}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.12}{\ignorespaces Detailed close-up view of the simulated magnetic field distribution at the top head region of the corroded rail profile, evaluated at an excitation frequency of \SI {50}{\hertz }.}}{61}{figure.caption.74}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.13}{\ignorespaces Detailed close-up view of the simulated magnetic field distribution at the bottom foot region of the corroded rail profile, evaluated at an excitation frequency of \SI {50}{\hertz }.}}{61}{figure.caption.75}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.14}{\ignorespaces Total magnetic field distribution for three representative corrosion defect sizes, illustrating the progressive increase in flux perturbation at the rail foot with growing defect volume.}}{62}{figure.caption.76}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.15}{\ignorespaces Finite-element simulation results comparing the induced magnetic field profiles across (a) the benchmark healthy track and (b) the track affected by structural foot corrosion (corrosion size: \SI {1}{\centi \meter }$\times $\SI {1}{\centi \meter }). A discontinuity of the magnetic field is observed in the area where the corrosion is located.}}{63}{figure.caption.77}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.16}{\ignorespaces Global magnetic field distribution for a fixed \SI {1}{\centi \meter }$\times $\SI {1}{\centi \meter } corrosion size, illustrating the progressive decay of the electromagnetic skin depth ($\delta $) across increasing excitation frequencies.}}{64}{figure.caption.79}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.17}{\ignorespaces Differential magnetic field magnitude $|\Delta B|$ evaluated at a fixed excitation frequency of \SI {50}{\hertz }, illustrating the spatial extent of the corrosion-induced anomaly and its rapid decay away from the defect zone.}}{65}{figure.caption.81}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.18}{\ignorespaces Schematic representation of the selected sensor placement. The OPM is located at a horizontal standoff of \SI {7}{\centi \meter } from the excitation coil, above the mechanical clearance line of the rail profile.}}{66}{figure.caption.83}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.19}{\ignorespaces Sensitivity plot of the differential field magnitude $|\Delta B(f)|$ as a function of excitation frequency for corrosion defects of varying lateral size. The dashed black line indicates the QuSpin noise floor (\SI {3}{\pico \tesla }). The shaded grey region marks the sensor's linear operating range ($B_{\mathrm {tot}} \leq \SI {150000}{\nano \tesla }$). The dashed orange line reports the total field magnitude $B_{\mathrm {tot}}$ at the sensor cutpoint, which remains just below the upper operating limit. Coil parameters: \SI {0.5}{\ampere }, 200 turns; sensor standoff: \SI {7}{\centi \meter }.}}{67}{figure.caption.85}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.20}{\ignorespaces Experimental setup used to detect the ground-state Hanle resonance in a cesium vapor cell.}}{69}{figure.caption.86}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.21}{\ignorespaces Lorentzian profiles for different DC field configurations: (a)~$B_y$ varied with $B_z = 0$, (b)~$B_z$ varied with $B_y = 0$, and (c)~$B_y$ and $B_z$ varied simultaneously.}}{70}{figure.caption.87}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.22}{\ignorespaces Raw data and Lorentzian fit of the ground-state Hanle resonance for the optimal DC field configuration ($B_y = \SI {0}{\nano \tesla }$, $B_z = \SI {4815}{\nano \tesla }$). The resonance amplitude is \SI {42.2}{\milli \volt }, the FWHM is \SI {8726.3}{\nano \tesla }, and the derived spin coherence lifetime is $\tau \approx \SI {0.52}{\milli \second }$.}}{71}{figure.caption.88}%
